Z||000006||theogre||05-25-2000||02:18 PM||||M.M. wood for a batery box isn't a good idea. by the time you get it thick enough to take the weight it's likely to get pretty ugly. (No, I don't just mean how it looks.)<P>wood also absorbs things. Even with a heavy polly coat it will likely crack at the corners and suck in water and acid which will quickly blow off the finnish.<P>stainless steel, aluminum, fiberglass, or a suitable blow/vac molded plastic are about the only choises.||||reg||1
Z||000007||batboy||05-25-2000||08:36 PM||||I agree with Boomer, beef up the one you have. Fierocious, so far I'm loving my RCC rear coil-overs. I pop riveted those brake lines to my metal box, but you probably better not do that with a fiberglass box. You might have to fabricate a bracket.||||reg||1
Z||000008||batboy||05-25-2000||08:37 PM||||Oh yeah, I reposted those battery box pics & moved the old topic up to the top again.||||reg||1
Z||000009||FIEROCIOUS||05-26-2000||01:41 AM||||Well it was my understanding that fiberglass doesn't stick well (adhesion) so I made the mold! Does anyone know what I can cover the wood with as a release agent? ||||reg||1
Z||000010||FIEROCIOUS||05-26-2000||01:42 AM||||batboy, how did you bend them? (brake lines) ||||reg||1
Z||000011||Fret Naught||05-26-2000||01:50 AM||||Release agent, Diesel fuel,kerosene, linseed oil all work well as a release agent. I prefer linseed oil||||reg||1
Z||000012||batboy||05-26-2000||09:13 AM||||In my case, I was just relocating the lines to a slightly different spot close by, so I didn't have to really rebend the lines much. What little I did rebend, I just carefully tweaked by hand. It's possible you may have to remove the lines and install longer ones to fit in your case. Using a cheap little tubing bender might help in that case.||||reg||1
Z||000013||Monkeyman||05-26-2000||10:06 AM||||The only reason I thought about making one out of wood was the cost.  I'm not the richest guy in the world right now (although I'm pretty sure I have the winning lottery ticket in my wallet <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">) so I'm trying to do things as cheaply as possible.  I see your point, ogre.  What about taking a plastic/fiberglass/?? battery box (maybe for a boat?) and cutting that down?  I'm sure I can make/find some decent looking brackets.  Would that work or do I really need an aluminum one?||||reg||1
Z||000014||batboy||05-26-2000||10:35 AM||||Monkey, I've seen some filmsy plastic "boat" battery boxes and I've seen some really nice heavy duty ones. I see no reason a guy couldn't make one of those boxes to work. Like you said, fabricate some hefty brackets, maybe out of angle iron and flat stock so the box will sit securely onto the bracket. I like that idea better than wood.||||reg||1
Z||000015||FIEROCIOUS||05-28-2000||02:39 AM||||I'm attempting my first box tomarrow I'll keep ya all posted! <BR>I'm going to use PVA for the release agent.<BR>does'nt Kinkos copy have digital cameras? I'll try to post pics! <BR>batboy, my box will sit about one inch higher in the compartment. also how does your box clear the sway bar????<BR>mine has a notch.<P>Any ideas on how to secure the battery? I noticed batboys has a bracket but this wont work on the fiberglass one.<P>How many layers thick should I go? The archie battery box is two layers thick?||||reg||1
Z||000016||batboy||05-28-2000||08:22 AM||||My box barely clears the sway bar, maybe 1/2 inch to spare. Can't you mold in some steel or aluminum predrilled narrow plates along the lip of the fiberglass or something? Or maybe make a bracket out of angle iron for the box to sit securely down on, like I described above? I don't know, I guess this is why I bought a pre-made box, so I wouldn't have to think so much.<p>[This message has been edited by batboy (edited 05-28-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000017||FIEROCIOUS||05-30-2000||02:14 AM||||batboy, I actualy pulled it off! I made a battery tray! It is far better than the V8 archie tray!<BR>I made it out of a "special" fabric which instead of telling you I'll try to post a pic! lets just say it matches the "black" plastic!<P>I've figured out the mounting problem and the box is installed however, how well does the black plastic hold up with all that weight? I used rivets and aluminum strips to mount my box. How much torsinal stress is put on the black plastic? any poblems yet?<P>Also was your box contured with the plastic panel mold? At the pasenger lower corner the black plastic has a shape molded in it. My box however is flat this causes a gap between the two,did you have simular problems? ||||reg||1
Z||000018||batboy||05-30-2000||03:42 PM||||Good going! I've wondered about the durability of that plastic spare tire well too. Since NHRA rules require relocated battery boxes to be bolted to the frame in two places, I'm going ahead and adding a couple pieces of 1" X 1/8" flat steel strips to tie in to the frame on either side for a little extra support. That should stop any flexing or bouncing from the battery weight. Additionally, I'll be legal if I want to take a couple runs down the dragstrip.||||reg||1
Z||000019||Motorhead||05-31-2000||04:53 AM||||Could I trouble someone for a drawing of box dimensions?  I'm working on a box made frome 10 ga. steel, bolted in.  Single plasma cut, bent and welded 1 piece unit.  A crude drawing with dimensions would save me lots of time.  Also, what gauge cable are you all using for that long run to the rear of the car? Where does this set-up ground to the chassis up front?  <P>Thanks,<P>Mike||||reg||1
